  "body": "I have recently addressed two bugs about radio and checkbox input types when detached from the DOM in chromium:\r\n1. [`input.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('click'))` should fire change events](https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=773680)\r\n2. [`input.click()` should NOT fire change events](https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=1051482)\r\n\r\nIn order to address the first bug, I made radios and checkboxes fire change events when detached from the DOM, which caused the second bug, which lead me to revert my change which introduced change events in these cases.\r\n\r\nI checked firefox and safari to see what they do, and these are my findings [with this test site](https://detached-change-event.glitch.me/) and the most up to date behavior I am about to set in chrome. Also, according to the reporter of the first bug I linked, IE would fire change events for dispatchEvent().\r\n| | Chrome | Firefox | Safari |\r\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `input.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('click'))` | no event | fires event | no event |\r\n| `input.click()` | no event | no event | no event |\r\n\r\nI failed to find anything relevant in the spec about this, and I also didn't see anything within the codebase to look for that would determine whether we are in the click() case or the dispatchEvent() case when emitting the change event.\r\n\r\nWhat should the behavior be? Can we add it to the spec?\r\n\r\ncc @mfreed7 @tkent-google ",
